    <DataNode elementId="AT4G29130-MONOMER" textLabel="Hexokinase-1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q42525" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="615" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; gene &lt;i&gt;HXK1&lt;/i&gt; was selected for its ability to complement the phenotype of the &lt;i&gt;hxk1 hxk2&lt;/i&gt; yeast double mutant, which completely lacks hexokinase (HXK) activity. The gene allowed the recombinant yeast strains to grown on media supplemented with fructose |CITS:[9014361]|. In spite of their ability to confer fructose utilization to the recombinant yeast, the enzyme encoded by this gene is unlikely to be a fructokinase as it is able to phosphorylate glucose (data not shown |CITS:[9014361]|). However, a later study showed that the enzyme was indeed able to catalyze the phosphorylation of both glucose and fructose |CITS:[10402427]|, although the enzyme shows a definite preference for the former.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G29130-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT4G29130.1" />
